  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how tumour heterogeneity may arise and be studied across primary, metastatic, and circulating disease. On the left, a primary solid tumour is shown with regions ranging from normoxic to hypoxic and necrotic. The hypoxic zone contains a highlighted clone of cancer cells that appears DNA repair-deficient and genomically unstable, suggesting that low-oxygen conditions foster aggressive subpopulations within the tumour.\n\nThis idea is extended to metastasis in the center, where a metastatic lesion in bone includes new hypoxic areas and unstable tumour cells, implying continued evolution, lineage plasticity, and genomic instability after spread. Along the bottom, systemic heterogeneity is represented by circulating tumour cells and other tumour-derived material in blood, such as ctDNA, proteins, or exosomes.\n\nThe right side outlines an analytical workflow for a solid tumour sample: histology and immunostaining, then spatial transcriptomics and spatial proteomics over a defined capture area with thousands of barcoded spots. A conjugated antibody panel includes markers linked to hypoxia, proliferation, metabolism, and DNA repair. These data feed into detection of chromosomal instability (CIN), using surrogate features such as centrosome amplification or micronuclei, followed by multi-omic bioinformatic analysis.",
